$ PEP 484 - Type Hints

PEP 484 defined *type → development/languages/Python/annotations*.

{ Annotated arguments and returns types stored in __annotations__

  At runtime, the annotated arguments and return types are accessible through `→ development/languages/Python/dunders/__annotations__`.

  The following script prints `{'t': <class 'str'>, 'n': <class 'int'>, 'return': None}`

code {
def f(t: str, n: int = 4):
    """Function that prints value of its first argument n times (the second argument)"""
    for _ in range(n): print(t)

# f('hello world')

print(f.__annotations__)
code }

}
{ No runtime checking

  Although the annotations are stored in `__annotations__`, no actual type checking takes place at runtime!

  In fact, the PEP explicitely states that
  "
    Python will remain a dynamically typed language, and the authors have no desire to ever make type hints mandatory, even by convention.
  "

}
{ Challenges

  A challenge with fully-annotated code is that it increases the number of required `import` statements which has been recognized by
    • PEP 563 (Postponed Evaluation of Annotations) and later also by
    • PEP 649 (Deferred Evaluation Of Annotations Using Descriptors)
 
? It seems that `typing.TYPE_CHECKING` was a result of these PEPs, but see also PEP 781 (Make `TYPE_CHECKING` a built-in constant)


}

